course: dictionary
lesson: 12707
title: Dominance: power and control
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Dominance means having more power, control or importance than others. «The dominant wolf led the pack and chose where they hunted.» The dominant wolf is the one in charge, so dominance is the power it has over the rest of the pack.
explain: Sports writers use dominance to say one team was much better than the other for a long time, not just for one lucky minute. «Our team showed total dominance in the second half and won 4-0.» Total dominance means they controlled the whole game.
explain: In science, dominance has a second meaning. In genetics, one version of a gene shows instead of another, so the dominant version is the one you see. The context tells you which meaning is which: animals and sport mean power, while genes mean which version shows.
explain: Check by swapping in a simple word. In «a position of dominance», you could say «a position of power» and the sentence still works. That is a good sign your meaning is right.
fact: Dominance is a noun that you cannot count, so we say «dominance», not «a dominance».
fact: «Dominance over» is followed by what is controlled, as in «dominance over the other teams».
fact: «Dominant» is the adjective, as in «the dominant wolf».
fact: In genetics, a dominant gene shows instead of a recessive one.
fact: «Total dominance» means complete control, with no real challenge from the other side.
exercise: «The dominant wolf led the pack.» What does dominant mean here? | Hungry and tired | *Most powerful and in charge | Lost in the snow | Very small and quiet
exercise: What does «total dominance» mean in a sports report? | A team that wears a red shirt | *Complete control of the whole match | Losing on purpose | Only a short break in play
exercise: Dominance means power or control. Which word is closest in meaning? | silence | cooperation | weakness | *control
test: In genetics, what does a dominant gene do? | *Shows instead of another version of the gene | Hides every other gene for ever | Is never seen at all | Turns into a recessive gene
test: «Our team had dominance over the other side all match.» What does this suggest? | The ball never left the goalkeeper | They lost every goal | *They were much stronger and in control | They went home at half time
exam: Which best explains dominance? | *Having more power, control or importance than others | Sleeping for a long time | A sudden accident on the field | Being very small
